                            {
                                "target_word": "gin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c907f1df-e4ff-364b-ad34-c229c0e87983",
                                        "entry_id": "d606a801-614b-33cc-a3d9-988c298c1fd0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To begin."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Early",
                                            "Modern",
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1916a987-2ad9-4506-a51a-b69183509424",
                                                "sense_id": "c907f1df-e4ff-364b-ad34-c229c0e87983",
                                                "text": "Gon. All three of them are deſperate : their great guilt / (Like poyſon giuen to worke a great time after) / Now gins to bite the ſpirits :[…]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "gin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                        "entry_id": "2f19858a-0e74-35ca-acad-42c4670cb0c6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An Aboriginal woman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "ethnic",
                                            "slur"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1428385a-6a0f-48d3-a3af-c9a96b159588",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "His next shot was discharged amongst the mob, and most unfortunately wounded the gin already mentioned ; who, with a child fastened to her back, slid down the bank, and lay, apparently dying, with her legs in the water."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8d0bd005-2552-4584-bd5a-ab622f2310ed",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "On December 28, in the same year [1828], he [John Allen] fought single handed a tribe of native blacks, numbering from thirteen to eighteen, besides \"gins\" to bring them spears, waddies, etc."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c17bb7ed-ec8d-4836-abe9-2308e123a0a1",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "1894, Ivan Dexter, Talmud: A Strange Narrative of Central Australia, published in serial form in Port Adelaide News and Lefevre's Peninsula Advertiser (SA), Chapter XXI, http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks16/1600641.txt\nFrom my position I could see the gins pointing back, and as the men turned they looked for a moment and then made a wild rush for the entrance."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c5c79d43-954c-4ee2-9dbb-c6e262a0ca75",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "How they must have laughed about the strutting of her whose mother was a wanton and aunt a gin!"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "244c714f-8a38-4719-af97-2bcc3100b35b",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "Dad said Shoesmith and Thompson had made one error that cost them their lives by letting the gins into the camp, and the blacks speared them all."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6c28c3f4-922e-465a-bc57-82652945cad4",
                                                "sense_id": "d62dfe16-3673-3a5c-97c1-301a5af199de",
                                                "text": "But there was this gin there, see, what they called a kitchen girl."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "gin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                        "entry_id": "8f009b40-3c02-3188-8bbc-7f801b5862f4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "If."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Appalachia",
                                            "Northern-England",
                                            "Scotland",
                                            "Southern-US"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2721a19-7ef9-4fb1-9ea6-377493797cc5",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "Gin the plough rests on the bank, / The loom, the nation, dies."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "44befc3b-9a55-4cc9-86d6-fc60307d91b9",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "An' gin I'm weel and can keep sober / You may look for it in October."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3cfaf80c-73c6-4832-82c2-ac4336d9276b",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "He's get han' and siller, / Gin he fancies me."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6be36d42-6dfc-4ff7-9958-f132d55f11fa",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "[…]for pronouncing according as one would ſay at London I would eat more cheeſe if I had it, the Northern man ſaith, Ay ſuld eat mare cheeſe gin ay hadet, and the Weſterne man ſaith Chud eat more cheeſe an chad it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c28f33bd-6014-47d9-b0c7-f31d2d2cbe19",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "yon felley at Barleigh has wrote farrantly (fairly) to my naunt; gin Robin could bur see ť letter he'd foind no fawt wi' me."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7043ab72-4b30-4c2c-aa08-7d7bf9865fbd",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "Wheeah, Ah thinks thee could, gin ye tried."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d5c2740b-34c4-490b-9706-786b8e43e654",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "\"Aw'd never ha slept i' mi bed gin that little un had bin dreawnded, an' me lookin' on loike a stump. Neay; that lass wur Bess, moi wench. We'n no notion wheer th' lad's mother is.\" Mr. Clough would have pressed the money upon him, but he put it back with a motion of his han."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "382f540d-c650-41b9-9569-91789bcaf72d",
                                                "sense_id": "80e0618f-aaa9-3226-a015-f7237703e2ca",
                                                "text": "[…] gin schoo sets off in a tantrum an' flaah's t'mistress wiv her blutherin […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e011aecf-d75a-47a1-9d50-3cbd3284a924",
                        "source_entry_id": "50e73c0b-6959-3a93-a165-df98b5d41d40",
                        "source_sense_id": "701eb776-1350-3a6b-a2f2-ec43856f5452",
                        "target_lang_word": "gin",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "gin",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                        "entry_id": "b341a4fb-9358-3572-85d3-9eb5b1752178",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Boisson alcoolisée aromatisée aux baies de genévrier."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dc189bd5-a984-40ea-98e8-c27b8cfbc7a8",
                                                "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                                "text": "Qu’il passât tout son temps dans sa cabine, nul n’y eût trouvé à redire, si le grossier master ne se fût pas grisé chaque jour de gin ou de brandy."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f9329e67-f74d-4e89-93a0-3c5a2f9eb9be",
                                                "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                                "text": "Je la pris dans mes bras, elle releva la tête en la balançant, puis elle me regarda, mais assurément sans me voir ; alors je respirai une odeur de genièvre qu’exhalait son haleine chaude. Je reculai. Elle laissa retomber sa tête sur ses deux bras étalés sur la table.\n— Gin, dit mon grand-père.\nEt il me regarda en ricanant, disant quelques mots que je ne compris pas."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2bfe1b50-01d0-4999-bb68-54f86c1ad53b",
                                                "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                                "text": "Alors il chercha l’oubli tour à tour dans le cidre normand, le poiré manceau, l’hydromel gaulois, le cognac français, le genièvre hollandais, le gin anglais, le wiskey écossais, le kirsch germain."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "317fc293-042c-4544-951a-28ab4a7c569f",
                                                "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                                "text": "C'est à partir du XVIᵉ siècle, par le biais des contacts avec les Européens au moment du commerce triangulaire, que les boissons distillées furent introduites en Afrique. Le gin et les autres boissons alcoolisées constituaient un mode de paiement pour les esclaves africains ([…])."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0f40dad-4725-4662-9c5d-7f566853918e",
                                                "sense_id": "6dbf534c-1b3a-30c1-957d-31274902833b",
                                                "text": "Un seul remède : cheuler. Il prend dans la portière une petite bouteille en plastique, moitié gin moitié Schweppes, […], et s'envoie une bonne rasade."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "591fe64b-67f3-4924-aeae-022fa17bdd8e",
                        "source_entry_id": "50e73c0b-6959-3a93-a165-df98b5d41d40",
